<refentry id="sql-refreshmaterializedview">
 <indexterm zone="sql-refreshmaterializedview">
  <primary>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</refname>
  <refpurpose>replace the contents of a materialized view</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW [ CONCURRENTLY ] <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable>
    [ WITH [ NO ] DATA ]
REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED VIEWS [ CONCURRENTLY ] [ WITH [ NO ] DATA ] [ VERBOSE ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> completely replaces the
   contents of a materialized view.  To execute this command you must have the
   <literal>MAINTAIN</literal>
   privilege on the materialized view.  The old contents are discarded.  If
   <literal>WITH DATA</literal> is specified (or defaults) the backing query
   is executed to provide the new data, and the materialized view is left in a
   scannable state.  If <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal> is specified no new
   data is generated and the materialized view is left in an unscannable
   state.
  </para>
  <para>
   <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> and <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal> may not
   be specified together.
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>ALL</literal> form refreshes every materialized view in the
   current database that the current user is permitted to refresh, rather than
   a single named materialized view.  Materialized views are refreshed in an
   order that respects dependencies between them: if one materialized view is
   defined in terms of another (directly, or through intervening ordinary
   views), the view it depends on is refreshed first.  A single
   <command>REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED VIEWS</command> therefore brings the
   entire chain up to date in one step.
  </para>

  <para>
   Materialized views that the current user does not own and does not have
   <literal>MAINTAIN</literal> privilege on are skipped rather than causing an
   error; the command succeeds even if it refreshes nothing.  If a refreshed
   materialized view depends, directly or through intervening views, on a
   materialized view that was skipped, it is refreshed against that
   dependency's current (possibly stale) contents.  Specify
   <literal>VERBOSE</literal> to see which materialized views were skipped.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ALL</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refresh all materialized views in the current database instead of a
      single named one.  When <literal>ALL</literal> is specified, no
      <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> is given.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refresh the materialized view without locking out concurrent selects on
      the materialized view.  Without this option a refresh which affects a
      lot of rows will tend to use fewer resources and complete more quickly,
      but could block other connections which are trying to read from the
      materialized view.  This option may be faster in cases where a small
      number of rows are affected.
     </para>
     <para>
      This option is only allowed if there is at least one
      <literal>UNIQUE</literal> index on the materialized view which uses only
      column names and includes all rows;  that is, it must not be an
      expression index or include a <literal>WHERE</literal> clause.
     </para>
     <para>
      This option can only be used when the materialized view is already
      populated.
     </para>
     <para>
      Even with this option only one <literal>REFRESH</literal> at a time may
      run against any one materialized view.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name (optionally schema-qualified) of the materialized view to
      refresh.  This is used only with the single-view form; it is not given
      with <literal>ALL</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>VERBOSE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Print a message for each materialized view as it is refreshed, and for
      each one that is skipped because the current user lacks permission to
      refresh it.  This option is only available with the
      <literal>ALL</literal> form.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   If there is an <literal>ORDER BY</literal> clause in the materialized
   view's defining query, the original contents of the materialized view
   will be ordered that way; but <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED
   VIEW</command> does not guarantee to preserve that ordering.
  </para>

  <para>
   While <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> is running, the <xref
   linkend="guc-search-path"/> is temporarily changed to <literal>pg_catalog,
   pg_temp</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED VIEWS</command> operates on the set of
   materialized views that exist when the command begins; materialized views
   created concurrently by other sessions after that point are not included.
  </para>

  <para>
   With <literal>ALL</literal>, <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal> applies to
   every materialized view the command would refresh: each one is left
   unpopulated and marked unscannable, so queries against it will fail until
   it is populated again by a subsequent <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED
   VIEW</command>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The command runs as a single transaction.  Without
   <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>, this means every refreshed materialized
   view is held under an <literal>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</literal> lock until the
   command commits, and all of the new contents are built before any of them
   become visible.  On a database with many or large materialized views this
   can be a long-running transaction holding many locks; consider refreshing
   selected materialized views individually if that is a concern.
  </para>

 <para>
   When <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> is specified, materialized views that
   cannot be refreshed concurrently &mdash; those that are not yet populated,
   or that lack a suitable unique index &mdash; are skipped with a warning and
   left unchanged.  Unlike a non-concurrent refresh, which populates an
   unpopulated materialized view, a concurrent refresh leaves it unpopulated.
   Consequently, a materialized view that reads from a dependency skipped for
   being unpopulated fails with an error, just as a stand-alone
   <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> reading from an unpopulated
   materialized view would; this rolls back the entire command.  To populate
   such materialized views, use the single-view form without
   <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>, or run <command>REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED
   VIEWS</command> without <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>, which populates
   them as it refreshes in dependency order.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   This command will replace the contents of the materialized view called
   <literal>order_summary</literal> using the query from the materialized
   view's definition, and leave it in a scannable state:
<programlisting>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW order_summary;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   This command will free storage associated with the materialized view
   <literal>annual_statistics_basis</literal> and leave it in an unscannable
   state:
<programlisting>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW annual_statistics_basis WITH NO DATA;
</programlisting></para>

  <para>
   Refresh every materialized view in the current database that the current
   user is permitted to refresh:
<programlisting>
REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED VIEWS;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Refresh them concurrently where possible, printing progress and skipping
   any that cannot be refreshed concurrently:
<programlisting>
REFRESH ALL MATERIALIZED VIEWS CONCURRENTLY VERBOSE;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

